.TH ENVIRON 5
.SH NAME
environ \- user environment
.SH DESCRIPTION
An array of strings called the "environment" is made available by
.BR exec (2)
when a process begins. By convention, these strings have the form "name=value". The following names are used by various commands:
.TP 8
.B PATH
The sequence of directory prefixes that
.BR sh (1),
.BR time (1),
.BR nice (1),
.BR nohup (1),
etc., apply in searching for a file are known by an incomplete path name. The prefixes are separated by colons (:).
.BR login (1)
sets \fBPATH=/bin:/usr/bin\fR.
.TP
.B HOME
Name of the user's login directory, set by
.BR login (1)
from the password file
.BR passwd (4).
.TP
.B TERM
The kind of terminal for which output is to be prepared. Some terminals supported are:
.nf
vi200            Visual Technologies
vi50
vt52             DEC
vt100
h19              Zenith
.fi
.PP
See a complete listing of terminals in \fB/etc/termcap\fR.
.TP
.B MAIL
If this variable is set to the name of a mail file, then the shell informs the user of the arrival of mail in the specified file.
.PP
Further names may be placed in the environment by the \fBexport\fR command and "name=value" arguments in
.BR sh (1).
.PP
It is unwise to conflict with certain shell variables that are frequently exported by \fB.profile\fR files: \fBMAIL\fR, \fBPS1\fR, \fBPS2\fR, \fBIFS\fR.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
login(1), sh(1), getenv(3C)
.sp
